CI/CD Pipeline with Jenkins + Docker

1. Set up Jenkins locally (using Docker)
    Note: Since I'm using macOS, I set up Jenkins using the Docker-in-Docker (DinD) concept.
    Use the Dockerfile inside the setup directory to build and run Jenkins.
    Run the following commands to build and start Jenkins:
        docker build -t doc-jenkins .
        docker run -d \
        --name jenkins-local \
        -p 8080:8080 -p 50000:50000 \
        -v jenkins_home:/var/jenkins_home \
        -v /var/run/docker.sock:/var/run/docker.sock \
        -u root \
        doc-jenkins
2. Once Jenkins is running, log in and install the necessary plugins.
3. Create a new folder, then create a pipeline linked to your Git repository.
4. Also, add Git credentials in Jenkins for authentication.
5. Configure the pipeline by adding the Jenkinsfile in the configuration section.
6. Finally, trigger the pipeline to start the build process.

    